[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Oracle Data Guard: Physical and Logical Standby Databases"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Oracle Data Guard ensures high availability, data protection, and disaster recovery for Oracle databases. It maintains one or more synchronized copies of a production database, known as the primary database. These copies are called standby databases. Data Guard supports two main types of standby databases: Physical and Logical."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Physical Standby Database"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A physical standby database is a block-for-block, physically identical copy of the primary database. It's maintained by applying redo data from the primary database to the standby database. This ensures that the physical structure and content of the standby database are consistent with the primary database."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Redo Apply: Redo data is transferred from the primary to the standby and applied to the standby database. This process keeps the standby database synchronized with the primary."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Recovery Mode: The physical standby database is typically in managed recovery mode, where the redo apply process is automatically managed by Oracle."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Read-Only Access: While in managed recovery mode, the physical standby database can be opened in read-only mode for reporting and testing purposes (Active Data Guard license required)."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Switchover and Failover: Physical standby databases can be used for switchover (planned maintenance) and failover (unplanned outage) operations to minimize downtime."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"-- Example: Starting Managed Recovery on a Physical Standby Database
ALTER DATABASE RECOVER MANAGED STANDBY DATABASE DISCONNECT FROM SESSION;
ALTER DATABASE RECOVER MANAGED STANDBY DATABASE USING ARCHIVED LOGFILE;
ALTER DATABASE RECOVER MANAGED STANDBY DATABASE CANCEL; -- To stop recovery"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Logical Standby Database"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"A logical standby database contains the same logical data as the primary database, but the physical structure and organization can be different. It's maintained by applying SQL statements that are generated from the redo data of the primary database. This allows for more flexibility in terms of database upgrades and reporting."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"SQL Apply: Redo data is transformed into SQL statements and applied to the standby database. This allows for more granular control over the replication process."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Open Read-Write: The logical standby database is open in read-write mode, allowing for reporting, testing, and even limited DML operations (with caution)."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data Divergence: Because the logical standby database is open for read-write, it's possible for the data to diverge from the primary database. Careful planning and monitoring are required to prevent data inconsistencies."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Supported Objects: Not all object types are supported for logical standby databases. Some object types may require special handling or may not be replicated."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"-- Example: Starting SQL Apply on a Logical Standby Database
ALTER DATABASE START LOGICAL STANDBY APPLY;
ALTER DATABASE STOP LOGICAL STANDBY APPLY;"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Comparison: Physical vs. Logical Standby"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Physical: Block-for-block copy, Redo Apply, faster recovery, simpler setup, requires Active Data Guard for read-only access."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Logical: Logical copy, SQL Apply, open read-write, more flexible, more complex setup, potential for data divergence."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuration Steps (Simplified)"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following steps outline a simplified configuration process. Refer to the Oracle documentation for detailed instructions."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"1. Prepare the Primary Database: Enable archiving, force logging, and configure the tnsnames.ora file."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"2. Create the Standby Database: Restore a backup of the primary database to the standby server or use RMAN duplicate."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"3. Configure Data Guard Broker (Optional but Recommended): The broker simplifies management and monitoring of the Data Guard environment."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"4. Start Managed Recovery (Physical) or SQL Apply (Logical) on the Standby Database."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Oracle Data Guard provides robust solutions for high availability and disaster recovery. Choosing between physical and logical standby databases depends on specific requirements, such as recovery time objectives (RTO), recovery point objectives (RPO), reporting needs, and acceptable levels of complexity. Understanding the characteristics of each type is crucial for designing an effective Data Guard configuration."}]
